  "opening": {
    "en": [
      {
        "title": "Starter Character Selection",
        "body": "Choose a character with straightforward kill confirms and clear recovery options for your first 50 hours: Mario, Pikachu, Wolf, or Lucina cover the widest skill-to-reward ratios. Avoid complex characters (Steve, Bayonetta, Pyra/Mythra) until you understand the universal mechanics of shield pressure, ledge options (2-frame punish, ledge trump, getup attack read), and stage positioning. Each of these fundamentals applies identically across all 89 fighters."
      },
      {
        "title": "Training Mode Priority",
        "body": "Spend the first 10 hours in Training Mode exclusively on two drills: perfect shield timing (shield release 1–2 frames before attack contact) and landing option mix-ups (fast-fall aerial, airdodge, drift away, drift in). These two mechanics appear in every match and their execution under pressure determines round outcomes more than character-specific combos. Set CPU to level 1 and practice perfect shield against 5 repeated moves before moving to matchup practice."
      }
    ]
  },
  "midGame": {
    "en": [
      {
        "title": "Stage Selection Strategy",
        "body": "Counter-picking stages is a legal and important part of competitive play. Flat stages (Battlefield, Final Destination) favour projectile characters and characters with strong horizontal kill power. Stages with platforms (Smashville, Town and City, Hollow Bastion) favour combo-heavy characters and those with strong up-air kill options. Learn your character's two best stages and one neutral stage, then select based on opponent character type."
      },
      {
        "title": "Ledge Option Coverage",
        "body": "Covering all four ledge options simultaneously is only possible with specific moves—identify which of your character's attacks covers 2–3 ledge options at once. Getup attack and normal getup share similar timing; roll is the most predictable option. The most common mistake at mid-level is placing hitboxes on the ledge rather than slightly away from it, which misses jump and roll options entirely. Position yourself one character-length from the ledge and time attacks to land as the opponent rises."
      }
    ]
  },
  "endGame": {
    "en": [
      {
        "title": "High-Level Neutral Game",
        "body": "At high play, neutral game is decided by micro-spacing and bait-and-punish rather than raw combo damage. Learn your character's best whiff-punish option (typically a fast dash attack or jab) and deliberately walk into range to bait approach attacks. The mental frame advantage system—acting as though you have an invisible timer on your actions—is more important than any specific technique. After landing a whiff-punish, immediately reset to neutral rather than overextending for a second hit."
      }
    ]
  },
  "mistakes": {
    "en": [
      "Mashing out of combos by pressing every button—button mashing increases DI randomness but does not guarantee escape; deliberate DI with a single correct direction outperforms mashing every time.",
      "Rolling away from the ledge as the first recovery option—back roll from ledge is the most predictable option and the easiest to punish; prioritise jump or neutral getup as default choices.",
      "Playing the same option repeatedly after it works once—opponents adjust within 2–3 repetitions; vary your approach mix with at least 3 different attack initiators to prevent reads.",
      "Ignoring percentage kill thresholds—most characters have consistent kill percentages for their key moves; if you don't know when your forward smash kills at centre-stage, you are leaving wins on the table.",
      "Neglecting recovery high vs. recovery low options—always vary your recovery height and angle; predictable recovery routes are punished by any player who recognises the pattern after two stocks."
    ],
